**1. What this project is**

A ceramics studio, of which software is only a part. From `PROJECT.md:14-15`:

> "Harbour Pottery is a ceramics studio. It makes and sells pottery, photographs it for a catalogue and runs a small shop site. Software is one part of it, not all of it."

**2. Things an agent must never do**

Nothing is forbidden. `PROJECT.md:17` records the absence explicitly:

> "Agent boundaries: none are currently recorded. No work is forbidden here today. If something looks outside this project, ask before starting it."

So on meeting work that looks outside the project, I would follow that same line — ask before starting it, rather than proceeding or refusing on my own judgement.

**3. A component, its address, and its registry limits**

`PROJECT.md:40-43`:

> "## Harbour Lookbook
>
> Assets. Live material. Work here as the task requires.
> Address: lookbook"

Its entry declares no limits on changing things there — "Work here as the task requires" is the only instruction, and no constraint accompanies it. The one caveat carried in the entry is that it is "Live material."

No component is listed but unattached: `lookbook/` exists and holds `a.jpg`. The reverse is true, though — `shop/src/app.js` exists on disk, and the shop site is described in `PROJECT.md:14` as part of the studio's work, but no `shop` component appears in the registry.

**4. Where a decision affecting two components is recorded**

`PROJECT.md:49`:

> "Project-wide documentation and decisions belong here, in `.docs/` and `.docs/decisions/`."

A decision spanning two components is project-wide, so it goes in `.docs/decisions/`. Note `PROJECT.md:51`: "`.docs/` does not exist yet." — the directory would have to be created.

**5. The file to read first**

`PROJECT.md`. Both `CLAUDE.md:3-4` and `AGENTS.md:3-4` say the same thing and point at it:

> "Read `PROJECT.md` in this folder before working anywhere in this project. It holds the principles, the agent boundaries and the registry of every component."

`PROJECT.md:3-5` confirms it from its own side: "**AI Project Entry Point** ... Read this before working anywhere in this project."
